      "writtenOn": "2026-02-02T15:42:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why make a string param and not just let the loader use the env variable?",
      "revId": "dee72348b6d699afff9c5bc2ca7c3dbf4f086743",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "49860526_97d4544a",
        "filename": "src/tint/cmd/fuzz/wgsl/fuzz.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 57,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000250
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-02-02T19:09:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Local user/dev safety mostly, this design requires explicit intent from the user to engage this fuzzing, so that the fuzzer doesn\u0027t just start sending garabage to their GPU if the env var is set when doing something like testing a CF test case.\n\nAdditionally this ensures when writting the rules for running things on CF which device is being tested is explicitly set, so the fuzzer doesn\u0027t accidently pick up something like a SwiftShader build used as fallback for something else.\n\n\nThinking on this a bit more it feels slightly more ergonomic (to me) for having it require it being explicitly set as a flag for this use case. I know one can write `VK_ICD_FILENAMES\u003d\u003cpath to mesa\u003e tint_wgsl_fuzzer` when running locally, but when running in GN/CF there will be a setup stage where the env vars are all listed, and then there will be the actual call being run, which will either be later in the log file or depending on the stages are run might be in a different log file. (If we were using this for multiple steps, instead of just running the fuzzer I would be more inclined to use the env var)",
      "parentUuid": "5a895167_8b9dbd0c",
      "revId": "dee72348b6d699afff9c5bc2ca7c3dbf4f086743",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
